<h2>The pure volume argument</h2>

<p>The most common objection to this approach is direct: "If I simply run 120 kilometers (75 miles) per week, at an easy pace most of the time, my muscular durability will be unbreakable on race day."</p>

<p>Physiologically, the argument is correct. Pure volume develops a dense capillary network, strengthens tendons through sub-maximal repetition, and creates a fatigue resistance that no isolated interval workout can replicate. This is exactly how professional sports operate.</p>

<p>The problem with this premise is not physiological; it is logistical and mechanical for the amateur. To jump from 60 km to 120 km (37 to 75 miles) per week without getting injured, you need time not just to run the extra hours, but to absorb the load. This means more sleep, impeccable nutrition, and less external stress.</p>

<p>When an athlete with a corporate routine tries to force 110 km (68 miles) a week just to gain durability, chronic fatigue sets in. Running mechanics worsen due to tiredness, ground contact time increases, and the load is transferred from the muscles to the joints and bones. The result is not durability; it is shin splints, plantar fasciitis, or a stress fracture weeks before the event.</p>

<p>Intelligent structuring—keeping volume where recovery allows, inserting race pace under fatigue in the long run, and keeping strength training strong—is not a shortcut. It is the optimization of durability for those living in the real world. The 26.2 miles do not just demand what you ran; they demand how you built your structural base.</p>
